  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Scattered afternoon/evening showers/storms today, \ncontinuing daily into next week.\n\n\nPockets of river valley fog this morning under mostly clear\nskies. Will see any fog dissipate an hour or two after sunrise.\n\nThe upper ridge of high pressure over the TN valley is forecast\nto weaken and retrograde farther west and become centered over \nthe Ark-La-Tex by the weekend. This will allow a front over the\nGreat Lakes to sag south over the Mid-Atlantic with increasing \nmoisture leading to a daily chance for showers and a few storms \npersisting through next week. Overall, severe threat remains \nlow given lack of shear to support organized deep convection. \n\nCoverage of any convection today should be rather \nlimited given the influence of upper ridging, but a lee side\ntrough may provide enough forcing by this afternoon for a few\nstorms to develop across the higher terrain. Should see storms\ndissipate with sunset, and then remain mostly dry again \ntonight. Better coverage of storms comes Friday as the front \nmoves closer to the forecast area and the ridge continues to \nshift west. As the front moves into the Carolinas, it stalls on\nSaturday and high pressure wedges south along the Appalachians,\nwhich again will provide a focus for widely scattered \nafternoon/evening rain/storms. The stalled front will lift \nnorth as a warm front on Sunday and multiple upper disturbances \nwill push through the region, continuing a daily chance for \nrain/storms into the middle of next week. \n\n\n\n\nWarmest temperatures will occur Thursday and Friday, featuring\nwidespread upper 80s to mid 90s. Will see temperatures lower \nsomewhat again over the weekend and early next week as the \nridging shifts west, but will still be above normal for mid\nSeptember.",
